From: surface-kit
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: simple: AWB from the brightest pixels on a grey locus; Windows colour calibration

Grey world is fooled by any scene dominated by one colour. Surface Pro 7
rear camera, 3100 K lamp, white paper as reference: grey world was 20%
off with the paper in view and 46% off on a teal-only scene (white paper
rendered pink, lime rendered yellow).

SwStats now also keeps colour sums of the unclipped sampled pixels per
luminance histogram bin. With a "locus" in the tuning file (sensor R/G,
B/G of neutral grey under known lights, warm to cool), Awb reads the light
colour from the brightest 2% of unclipped pixels, snaps it onto the
locus and smooths it over frames. Errors in the same two scenes: 3% and
6%. Without a locus the behaviour is unchanged.

Tuning (both Surface Pro 7 cameras, from the Windows driver's module
calibration OV8865_MSHW0191_ICL.aiqb / OV5693_MSHW0190_ICL.cpf): grey
locus, colour matrices for A/D50/D65/D75 keyed by the colour temperature
the IPA's estimateCCT() reports for those lights, and AGC target 1.6 with
contrast 1.4 (the old 2.2 / 1.0 left no pixel darker than mid grey).

